215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 721 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables to give names to pin numbers so they are easy to use in the code. For example, we can define an LED pin.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 722 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only one time when the Arduino starts. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell whether a pin will work as an input or an output.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 723 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs again and again continuously. This allows the Arduino to keep checking or controlling the circuit without stopping.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 724 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we use digitalWrite\(\) to turn an output ON or OFF. For example, HIGH turns the LED ON and LOW turns it OFF.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (So overall, setup\(\) prepares everything once, and loop\(\) keeps controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
