/P 746 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables to give names to pin numbers so they are easy to use in the code. For example, we can define a button pin and an LED pin.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 747 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only one time when the Arduino starts. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell whether a pin will work as an input or an output.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 748 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs again and again continuously. This allows the Arduino to keep checking inputs and controlling the circuit without stopping.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 749 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, digitalRead\(\) is used to check the state of a button or switch \(HIGH or LOW\). Based on this input, we use an if condition to make decisions. For example, if the button is pressed, we can turn an LED ON using digitalWrite\(\), otherwise we turn it OFF.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
