458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 799 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables to give names to pin numbers so they are easy to use in the code. For example, we can define a button pin and an LED pin.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 800 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only one time when the Arduino starts. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell whether a pin will work as an input or an output.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 801 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs again and again continuously. This allows the Arduino to keep checking inputs and controlling the circuit without stopping.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 802 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, digitalRead\(\) is used to check the state of a button or switch \(HIGH or LOW\). Based on this input, we use an if condition to make decisions. For example, if the button is pressed, we can turn an LED ON using digitalWrite\(\), otherwise we turn it OFF. We can also use Serial lines like Serial.println\(\) to display values or messages in the Serial Monitor.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (So overall, setup\(\) prepares everything once, and loop\(\) keeps reading inputs, controlling outputs, and showing information continuously.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
